  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nWe're already seeing an offshore pressure gradient from San Diego to \nTonopah, NV. As of 11 AM there were weak north to northeast winds \nbeing observed with gusts mainly 20 to 25 mph, a few local gusts to \n30 mph in windier locations. For Thursday, an upper level high will \ndevelop over Northern California, with low pressure retrograding \nback towards Southern California. That will strengthen the offshore \npressure gradient and increase winds. For Friday and Saturday, that \nlow will remain just southwest of San Diego off the coast of \nnorthern Baja, maintaining a strong offshore pressure gradient. \n\nWinds on Thursday will be mainly out of the north/northeast, with \nthe strongest winds on the foothills and below passes in the San \nBernardino and Santa Ana mountains. Winds are expected to be \nstrongest and most widespread Friday and they develop more of an \neasterly component. The ECMWF Extreme Forecast Index on Friday has \ngusts in the 80th to 90th percentile and locally into the 95th to \n99th percentile. Wind gusts have the potential to exceed 65 mph in \nthe typically windy spots like near the Cajon Pass, in wind prone \ncanyons in the Santa Ana mountains, and in wind prone spots in the \nSan Diego mountains. Santa Ana winds could extended to coastal areas \non Friday, with a 30 to 50 percent chance of local wind gusts \ngreater than 35 mph. Winds become weaker and easterly on Saturday, \nwith gust 30 to 40 mph in the valleys and below passes, and gusts up \nto 50 mph on mountain foothills and below passes. Weaker offshore \nwinds may continue on the coastal mountain slopes Sunday with \nonshore flow expected to return by Monday. \n\nThe location of the upper level low to the southwest of San Diego, \nwill help keep relative humidity elevated and could elevate chances \nof showers in the deserts. Right now chances are 10 percent or less, \nwith low confidence in measurable rain. \n\nHigh temperatures on Thursday will be a few degrees below average \nfor the mountains and valleys with coastal areas as much as 12 to 18 \ndegrees above average. High temperatures on Friday will be a few \ndegrees warmer for most locations, bringing highs closer to 15 to 20 \ndegrees above average for coastal locations. Moderate HeatRisk is \nexpected for the coast into the western San Diego County valleys for \nThursday through Saturday with Major HeatRisk for some coastal areas \non Friday. Conditions will remain above average but cool a few \ndegrees on Saturday with continued cooling west of the mountains \ninto next week. \n\nWe're monitoring the potential for precipitation Tuesday and \nWednesday of next week. There remains differences in the models with \nthe GEFS guidance trending more inside and the ECMWF showing a more \nwesterly track. While there remains the most uncertainty in the \nprecipitation forecast, there is higher confidence in cooler and \nwindier conditions.",
